```java
class Coffee {}
class CoffeeWithMilk extends Coffee {}
class CoffeeWithSugar extends Coffee {}
class CoffeeWithMilkAndSugar extends Coffee {}
class CoffeeWithMilkAndSugarAndWhip extends Coffee {}
// ... one class per combination
```

With 4 independent toppings (milk, sugar, whip, syrup), a full combination hierarchy needs up to 16 classes — and every new topping doubles that count again. This is the concrete number an interviewer wants you to state, not just gesture at.

```java
interface Beverage { double cost(); }
class Coffee implements Beverage { public double cost() { return 2.0; } }

abstract class Addon implements Beverage {
    protected final Beverage base;
    Addon(Beverage base) { this.base = base; }
}
class Milk extends Addon {
    Milk(Beverage b) { super(b); }
    public double cost() { return base.cost() + 0.5; }
}
```

`new Milk(new Sugar(new Coffee()))` composes any combination of the 4 toppings from just 5 classes total (Coffee + 4 add-ons), not 16 — and a 5th topping adds one class, not a doubling.

Decorator strains when one wrapper needs a capability the shared `Beverage` interface never declared — say `WhipCream` needs a `meltsAfterSeconds()` method that plain `Coffee` doesn't have.

```java
interface Beverage { double cost(); }
interface Meltable { int meltsAfterSeconds(); }

class WhipCream extends Addon implements Meltable {
    public int meltsAfterSeconds() { return 900; }
}
```

Callers now need to check `instanceof Meltable` (or a safe cast) to reach the extra method — a sign that decorator composition is starting to leak the concrete wrapper types it was supposed to hide.
